Z||000000||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-24-2000||02:27 AM||||First of all  the car's that the question's are about are my totalled 87GT and a 86SE 2m6...  I'm taking the motor out of the GT  and putting it in the SE...<BR>ok....<BR>Number1..<BR>I noticed that the oil pan drain plug's are different..  hhmm    so do I need to put the 86 oil pan  on the 87 motor???  Is it different because there was a change in the cradle or what????<BR>will the 87 oil pan work in there??<BR>ok...<BR>Number 2...<BR>The upper and middle on the 86 motor was extremly clean(no carbon at all).. and the intakes on the 87 motor has about a 1/8 of an inch of carbon build up on them... YUCKO...   <BR>So the 86 intakes will fit the 87 right??  (I noticed that on the bottom of the upper intakes, that the number's are different..  hhmm<P>Number 3...<BR>A tranny(auto's) Question...<BR>Do I need to keep (use) the 87 Torque Converter with the 87 motor(balanced)??  Or can I just drop it in the 86 and use the 86 torque on the 87 motor???  Ipulled the motor out of the top...  was real easy....<P>Were those stupid question's??  lol<P>Thanks...   Steve  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||11-24-2000||03:18 AM||||There are no stupid questions.....it's better safe than sorry!<P><BR>1.  The pan and timing chain cover were redesigned in '87, so it looks different than the one in your '86.  No need to swap pans.....it will be OK (plus you'd have to swap over the timing cover too).<P>2.   The intakes were identical from '85-'88, so no problems swapping these around.  The only difference was the addition of the engraved "FIERO" on the upper plenum ('87-'88 only). <P>3.  The torque convertor has nothing to do with whether or not an engine is internally balanced or not, so there is no need to mess with it. <P>BTW.....only  the '88 V-6 was internally balanced.  The flexplate/flywheel is different on the '88 engines, so if you were to put an '88 engine into an earlier Fiero you would need the flywheel/flexplate from the '88 engine.  Conversely, if you were to put in an early V-6 into an '88 you would need the flywheel/flexplate from the early motor.  <P><BR>You may just want to install the entire cradle assembly from the '87 GT into the '86 SE (unless it was damaged in the wreck).  It would save you a lot of work, plus the '87 has a different final drive ratio than the '86 (3.33 versus 3.06).  This will give you slightly better acceleration, etc., but mainly it would be easier for you to just unbolt one cradle and install the other.<P>Either way, good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 11-24-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-24-2000||11:24 AM||||MR FIERO..  Thank's for the reply...<BR>It would have been easier to just switch cradles ,,,,  BUT what I'm doing is working on a 3.4 turbo setup with a 5 speed in it to put in the SAME SE...  Going to do that in a month or so...  I JUST NEED (BADLY) TO DRIVE A OR ANY Fiero TO WORK EVERYDAY... I'M ADDICTED TO THEM I GUESS.... <BR>That's why I did'nt switch the 87 tranny(better ratio) right now....  <BR>I CAN'T WAIT FOR THE TURBO... I was talking to seanh and 1FST2m6 about the possibility of going with a TWIN TURBO setup... So that IS still a possibility(smiling)lol...  when I get through with it , it is going to be a blast.... torque,torque,torque....  <BR>                          Steve||||reg||12
